WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:06.350
KEITH DANEKIND: Hi.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.500
I'm Keith Danekind.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.600
Welcome to Cloud OnAir, live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.150
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.733
And today, I'm going
to talk about how

00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:17.130
to connect APIs with existing
identity management systems.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.490
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.180
and we'll have Googlers
standing by to answer them.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.589
So let's get started.

00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:25.880
So here's our agenda for today.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.700
I'm going to do a brief
overview of Apigee Edge

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.500
so you can see what
that looks like.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.659
Then I'm going to talk
about identity management

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:35.930
and how you can integrate
that with your existing APIs.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.990
So how would you take
your existing APIs

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.880
and add them so that
they can authenticate

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.800
against your identity
management system,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.899
both on the front
end on the back?

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:46.690
After that, we'll do
questions and answers.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.457
So let's get started with
an overview of Apigee Edge

00:00:51.457 --> 00:00:53.040
and what that is at
a very high level.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.270
So this describes the
philosophy for Apigee Edge.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.170
Apigee has seen a repeated
pattern of success

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.128
with its customers, and
it's driven by how they

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:05.430
think about their API program.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.680
And it requires a shift in
how you think about this.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.780
So traditionally,
we think about this

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.100
from an exposure point of view.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.010
You can just expose a
service and be finished.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.320
But thinking about it from
the end user's point of view

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.550
leads to much better results.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.140
Our successful customers
start from the user,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.540
thinking about what that
user's experience will be like

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.980
and then working
backwards from there.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.250
Providing the application
developer with great APIs

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.870
will reduce the complexity
of creating new applications.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.390
The developer is free to
focus on user experience

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.910
and iterate on a few
different designs.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.070
The API team makes this
possible by providing

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.100
easy to use APIs with consistent
interfaces and security.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.900
The API team builds an API
facade on existing services,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.600
abstracting away
the complexities.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.680
Hide away all that
enterprise stuff.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.110
Apigee Edge makes it
easy for your API team

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.400
to build great APIs.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.470
And Apigee Edge also makes
it easy for developers

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.510
to consume those APIs.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.040
It also lets you measure every
aspect of your API program.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.000
So here's Apigee Edge.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.210
Let's talk about what's inside.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.280
Apigee Edge is a single platform
with three conceptual parts.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.070
API Services allows you to
expose and manage services

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:13.720
to an API.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.520
Developer Services
provides a portal

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.680
where application
developers or API consumers

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.734
can learn about your APIs
and request access to them.

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:22.900
And Analytics Services lets
you measure every aspect

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.027
of your API program.

00:02:24.027 --> 00:02:26.110
So let's look at these
three parts in a little bit

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.530
more detail.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.526
So for API Services, this
is where we build APIs.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:32.900
So this is the
bulk of what we're

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.635
going to be doing when we think
about integrating with things.

00:02:36.635 --> 00:02:38.510
On the left we're going
to have applications,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.790
maybe a mobile application,
a web application,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.770
or some sort of partner system.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.850
These applications
make API calls.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.600
They're going to send
HTTP requests in.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.940
Apigee Edge lets you pass
that request straight

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.242
through the back-end system.

00:02:51.242 --> 00:02:52.700
Or you can change
anything about it

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.680
in the headers, the
URI, or the body.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.170
You can even do intelligent
routing based on the caller.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.836
Maybe there's different
back-end systems

00:02:58.836 --> 00:03:00.195
you want to send them to.

00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:01.820
You can also manage
the flow of traffic

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.067
by imposing rate
limits or quotas.

00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:05.150
And you can apply caching.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.310
So you have complete
control over the request

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.350
and the reply in there.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.331
When the back-end system
returns a response,

00:03:11.331 --> 00:03:13.580
you can send that response
straight to the application

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:14.859
or change it around as needed.

00:03:14.859 --> 00:03:16.400
For example, you
might want to reduce

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.820
the payload to a mobile client
or transform XML into JSF.

00:03:22.207 --> 00:03:23.665
So after you build
some great APIs,

00:03:23.665 --> 00:03:25.600
you want to make it
easy for your developers

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.180
to discover them and
start using them.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.310
The developer portal
gives you a place

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.140
to put your documentation
and tools that

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.570
let your developers
register for access.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.220
Developers can try out your
APIs from the documentation.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.310
They can fill in data
fields, see what the request

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.816
and response looks like.

00:03:40.816 --> 00:03:42.190
And the Developer
Portal is going

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:45.040
to greatly reduce the friction
when getting started with APIs.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.290
So this is your one stop
shop for all your developers.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.831
Rather than having
meetings with them

00:03:48.831 --> 00:03:51.430
or sharing PDF documents
or anything like that,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.320
you've got a nice
website where they can go

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.930
and they can get
registered and see docs.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.672
So after you built a few APIs
and your application developers

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:02.880
have discovered them and
started to send you traffic,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.650
it'd be great if you could
measure your API program.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.110
The analytics system
in Apigee Edge

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.690
allows you to measure every
aspect of your API traffic.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.400
You can measure
operational details

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.984
like response time and frequency
of different types of errors.

00:04:13.984 --> 00:04:16.400
You can find things that are
interesting to your business,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.660
like where are people located
when they call your APIs

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.059
or which applications are
sending the most traffic?

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:23.100
Maybe it's interesting to
see what type of client

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:23.820
they're using.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:24.960
Are they on Chrome?

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.280
Or are they on Firefox?

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.320
Are they on iOS or Android?

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.710
You can get details like
that in Analytics Services.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.710
And you can add your own
custom metrics in here.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.835
So if you want to measure
something that's specific

00:04:34.835 --> 00:04:37.405
to your program, grab that out
of the request or the response

00:04:37.405 --> 00:04:39.780
and connect that with some of
the existing out-of-the-box

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.060
analytics and get full
detailed reports on that.

00:04:44.572 --> 00:04:46.030
So let's talk about
our demo today.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.590
We're going to talk about how
to integrate with an IDM system

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.600
or identity management system.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.490
So there's a few types
of authentication

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.940
we might need to consider
when we're building APIs.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.380
We may just need
to identify which

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.270
application's calling our API.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.930
Is it a website application?

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:02.880
Is it an iOS app?

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.694
Is it an Android app?

00:05:04.694 --> 00:05:07.360
But in addition to knowing which
application is calling our API,

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.360
we might need to authenticate
the end user who's

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.860
actually using the application.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.000
Maybe we've got
something in there

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.291
that's a little more sensitive.

00:05:13.291 --> 00:05:16.240
Maybe It's a banking application
or something like that.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.640
Also, our back-end
services might

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:18.870
require some authentication.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.411
And that might be
different from what

00:05:20.411 --> 00:05:22.077
we're doing with the users.

00:05:22.077 --> 00:05:23.910
So in a lot of cases,
we'll want to separate

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.285
front-end authentication
from back-end authentication.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:29.410
We might have a variety of
different authentication

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.540
mechanisms on the
back side, but we

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.580
want to provide a
single consistent way

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.489
of authenticating
when you call an API.

00:05:35.489 --> 00:05:37.030
We want to hide away
the gory details

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.217
of the back-end authentication
from our API consumers.

00:05:40.217 --> 00:05:42.550
And you might have multiple
ways of authenticating calls

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.966
to that back-end
system, depending

00:05:43.966 --> 00:05:47.440
on which back-end system you're
calling and when it was built.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.690
So we want to provide a
facade on the front that gives

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.525
consistency to all of the APIs.

00:05:54.114 --> 00:05:55.530
So let's consider
an example where

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.640
you need to authenticate
end users using

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.640
an external identity
management system

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.260
and call a back-end service
that uses a service account that

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.240
requires a username and
password in the form

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.022
of basic authentication.

00:06:06.022 --> 00:06:08.230
We'll prompt the user for
their username and password

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.840
and we'll store the username
and password in the back end

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.860
in Apigee's key value map.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.980
So in this case,
what we're doing

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.560
is, on that
left-hand side there,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.470
we're going to ask them for
a username and password.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.136
And on the right-hand side, it's
a completely different username

00:06:21.136 --> 00:06:24.280
and password to get
into that system.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.630
The identity management
system could be anything.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.047
It might be an
off-the-shelf thing

00:06:28.047 --> 00:06:29.421
that you've already
got in place.

00:06:29.421 --> 00:06:31.900
It might be a cloud-based
identity management system.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.430
Might be your corporate LDAP.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.270
In this case, I'm
making it generic

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.300
so we can look at examples
of how this can work.

00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:41.900
So here's what our
authentication flow

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.260
might look like.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.910
Let's consider a request.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.847
Let's consider a request
from the users in there.

00:06:48.847 --> 00:06:51.180
Now look at the application
on the left-hand side there.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.802
Let's look at the
business flow first.

00:06:52.802 --> 00:06:54.260
When the user comes
in, they're not

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.330
going to have an
authentication token.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:59.360
So they would go down to
that authentication flow

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.190
and get a token,
The authentication

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.930
flow would talk to an
identity management system.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:07.100
And then, at the end of that,
it's going to generate a token.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:10.330
So if you've got that token,
you flow through that top path

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:10.870
easily.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.857
So that token is sent
as a request header.

00:07:13.857 --> 00:07:15.190
We're going to check that token.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:16.930
We're going to apply a
little bit of traffic control

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.359
in the form of
that spike arrest.

00:07:18.359 --> 00:07:20.150
That will keep us from
calling too quickly,

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.077
keep the back end from
getting overwhelmed.

00:07:22.077 --> 00:07:23.660
Then we're going to
remove the header.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.160
And that's going to remove that
security header because it's

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.690
not required for the back end.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.530
We'll grab the credentials
for the back end

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.030
using the key value map.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:33.580
And then we'll set a new
basic Auth header in there

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.380
before it goes to the back
end using that identity.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.560
So one identity on
the left-hand side

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.900
in the form of that OAuth
token, and one identity

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.609
on the right-hand side in the
form of a user basic header.

00:07:45.609 --> 00:07:46.900
So where do you get that token?

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.850
That's where the authentication
flow on the bottom comes in.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.990
So let's assume the
application connects.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:52.990
It doesn't have a token.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.450
It's going to get
back an HTTP 401.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:57.670
And that means they're
not authorized.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:00.520
So that application will know
to call out to a token flow

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.562
where it can get
an access token.

00:08:02.562 --> 00:08:05.020
So we pass in a username and
password to the authentication

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.390
flow.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:09.720
That is going to call out to
an identity management system.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.370
So that first blue
arrow there is

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.260
a call-out to identity
management system.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.530
And we're going to assume it
has an HTTP end point on it.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.300
So we call out to that.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.840
If the username and password is
good, it'll return a 200 to us.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.680
And if that works,
we'll continue.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.749
If not, that second thing in
there, BadAuth, will fire.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:28.290
And that will return
an error letting

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.706
the application know that the
username and password wasn't

00:08:30.706 --> 00:08:31.690
good.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:32.520
So if that does
work, we're going

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.929
to collect some things from
the identity management system.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.470
There might be some things in
there about what their role is.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.600
And we could use that
later on for authorization,

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.224
figuring out what
they're allowed to do

00:08:41.224 --> 00:08:43.570
and what they're
not allowed to do.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.250
From there, we'll
look up the keys

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.710
that are specific to this API
that the user is requesting

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:48.484
a token for.

00:08:48.484 --> 00:08:49.900
And then we'll
generate the token.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.399
And that's an OAuth token that
will be returned to the user.

00:08:52.399 --> 00:08:55.390
So then that's returned
up to the application,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.590
and the application
then has the token.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.750
So if you want an
analogy for this,

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.920
think about when you go to a
festival or something like that

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.140
and you're given a wristband.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.014
And that wristband might
be a different color

00:09:06.014 --> 00:09:08.650
depending on what day it is
or what you're allowed to do.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.020
Maybe folks over 21
are given one color

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:12.820
because they're allowed
to go and drink,

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:14.920
and other folks are
given a different color

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.000
because they're not.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.220
So there's a credential
that's presented

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.990
in the form of a driver's
license or something like that.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:23.342
And then that is
exchanged for a token,

00:09:23.342 --> 00:09:24.550
which would be the wristband.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.590
And the wristband gives
you different entitlements

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:27.610
based on what it looks like.

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:29.140
We can use that
analogy to describe

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:30.431
what this flow would look like.

00:09:32.709 --> 00:09:35.000
So let's dig into the API
proxy and see what that looks

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.890
like inside of Apigee Edge.

00:09:43.944 --> 00:09:44.860
So here's Apigee Edge.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.510
If you haven't seen this
before, let me jump out

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:47.843
and give a little bit of a tour.

00:09:52.714 --> 00:09:55.380
So when we first come into here,
we're greeted with this screen.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.700
This is where we're going
to build API proxies.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.080
So in this case, we're playing
the role of the API developer.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.790
We also have
application developers

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.290
that'll consume our APIs.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.250
That's a different
group of developers.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.610
So let's focus on
API development.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.110
What I would do
when I come in here,

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.526
I'm going to click
on API Proxies.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.800
I've got a list in
here of APIs proxies.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:18.216
If I want to build a new one,
I can come up to this button

00:10:18.216 --> 00:10:20.260
and hit Plus API Proxy.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.390
A few different
options in there.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.520
Most commonly, I'm going
to build a reverse proxy.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:25.990
I might have a spec for it.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:28.330
If I have an open API or
Swagger spec for that,

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:31.640
I could build my API
proxy using that.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:32.950
Maybe I've got a SOAP Service.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.934
I could come down and use that.

00:10:34.934 --> 00:10:37.225
There's a wizard in there to
convert SOAP to REST, REST

00:10:37.225 --> 00:10:37.930
to SOAP.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:40.320
I can digest a Whistle, and
it'll make a conversion thing

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.840
in there.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.399
No Target allows us to create
a stubbed out interface.

00:10:44.399 --> 00:10:46.190
So we're going to come
up with the response

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.990
right there in the proxy.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:49.730
We've got Hosted
Functions and Node.js.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:52.730
They're both going to allow us
to host something in Node.js

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.530
inside of Apigee Edge.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:58.920
And then Proxy Bundle would let
us import an existing zip file.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.480
So in most cases, I'm
going to do reverse proxy.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:03.000
We come in here.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.240
Proxy name, base path,
and then an existing URL.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.870
So I would walk
through this wizard.

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.010
And then, at the end of it, I
end up on a development screen.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.060
I'm going to show you
guys a pre-built one.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.995
So let's go back and look at
our identity management example.

00:11:26.534 --> 00:11:28.450
So in this case, I'm
calling a back-end system

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:29.770
that's an employee directory.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.964
And it requires a
username and password.

00:11:32.964 --> 00:11:34.630
And it's a system
username and password.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:36.850
It's just basically
a service account.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.550
But on the other side, I
want to authenticate my users

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:42.150
against our identity
management system.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.150
So I would end up on a
summary screen like this.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.630
This is the URL that I'll call.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.171
And then, this is
the back-end system

00:11:47.171 --> 00:11:49.730
that it's going
to call by proxy.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:51.530
We'll click over on
the Develop tab here

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:53.610
and we'll see what's
going on in there.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.476
So here's the flow I was
talking about before.

00:11:56.476 --> 00:11:59.100
What we're going to see in here
is there's a number of policies

00:11:59.100 --> 00:11:59.760
up here.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.800
And then we've got
some code down here

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.030
on the bottom that configures
what those policies do.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.732
So policies that are available.

00:12:06.732 --> 00:12:08.690
This is the request flow
and the response flow.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:10.700
We can put whatever
we like in here.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.560
If I click up here,
we'll see that there's

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.870
a number of policies
for traffic management,

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:18.080
for response caching,
working with caches,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.709
populating them, invalidating
them, warming them

00:12:20.709 --> 00:12:23.000
up, that sort of thing, all
kinds of things with cache,

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.190
either through a policy
or programmatically

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:26.630
through an API call.

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.220
Lots of policies
around security.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.536
We're going to use the
OAuth policy in here.

00:12:30.536 --> 00:12:31.910
A lot of things
around mediation.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.190
So converting between formats,
returning custom error

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.090
messages, things like that.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:37.469
And then custom extensions.

00:12:37.469 --> 00:12:40.010
So if we want to do things with
Java or Python or JavaScript,

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:40.820
we can do that.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.310
And I'm going to make use of
the service call-out in here

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:47.110
to call out to our
identity management system.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:49.180
So let's go through here
and see what's in here.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.060
So first thing
I've got in here is

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.160
I'm going to check and make sure
that they have an access token.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:56.830
So I want to verify their access
token and make sure it's valid.

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:59.174
So if it's not valid,
we're going to send back

00:12:59.174 --> 00:13:00.090
an unauthorized error.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.423
And if it is valid, flow will
continue over to the right

00:13:02.423 --> 00:13:03.910
here.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:07.054
So first thing to do is
we've got an OAuth 2.0

00:13:07.054 --> 00:13:07.720
policy in there.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.150
And then, this configures it.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.650
I've got it in Verify
Access Token mode.

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:13.950
There's a bunch of different
operations it can do.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:18.500
And I've chosen to have
it on Verify Access mode.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:19.730
Spike Arrest is next.

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.400
So Spike Arrest
will reduce or it'll

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:25.010
limit the frequency at
which somebody can call.

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:27.530
So I've limited this
to 10 calls per minute.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:29.570
If they call faster
than that, I'm

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:31.400
going to return
an error message.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.680
You might also use a
quota policy in here.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.280
That's a fixed number of calls.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:38.000
So one would control the
rate at which you can call,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.370
and the other would be a
fixed quota of number of calls

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:42.107
per day, per hour, per minute.

00:13:42.107 --> 00:13:43.940
The quota tends to be
more business related,

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.590
what you've agreed
with the customer.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:47.330
And then the spike
arrest is really just

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:49.038
to protect your systems
and keep somebody

00:13:49.038 --> 00:13:51.657
from just hammering the system.

00:13:51.657 --> 00:13:53.990
Next thing I'm going to do
in here is remove the header.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.760
So we've sent in an
authorization header

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:56.852
with that token.

00:13:56.852 --> 00:13:59.060
But we don't want to send
that to the back-end system

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:01.280
because it doesn't understand
it, it doesn't need it,

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:02.356
anything like that.

00:14:02.356 --> 00:14:03.980
So what this is going
to do is actually

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:05.780
remove the header
from the request

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:08.720
before it sends that request
onto the back-end system.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.420
So remove the
authorization header.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:14.060
And the next one is we're
going to do Get Credentials.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:17.330
So Get Credentials in here,
what that's going to do

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:18.980
is it's a key value
map operation.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:21.950
And we're going to look up
the username and the password

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.300
for the back-end system.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.980
So we'll have those as
variables in the system,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.820
and then we can use
them in the next step.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:30.410
These are stored
in a key value map.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:32.540
So there's an encrypted
key value map in here.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.390
And that's under
the Environment tab.

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:35.810
We'll find that
over on this side.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.940
And I'll take a
look at that later.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.520
This will set a basic
authentication header.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.520
So we're going to take
that username and password

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.900
that we got in
the previous step,

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.940
and we're going to create
a basic Auth header.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.690
So that's a base
64 encoded string

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.150
of the username and password.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.900
And that's what our
back-end system understands.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.710
And then, that goes to
the back-end system.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:58.680
And we should be logged in
and we should get data back.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.420
I'm not doing any
processing on the return,

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:02.711
but I could reformat the
response or anything like that

00:15:02.711 --> 00:15:03.920
if I want.

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:06.754
So let's take a look
at this step here.

00:15:06.754 --> 00:15:08.420
Where did we get these
credentials from?

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.630
So let's go over
here and take a look.

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.000
I'm going to click on
Environment over here.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.850
And what we'll see
in here is that we've

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.660
got a few different settings.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.410
So we've got different
caches we can have.

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.560
They can be associated with
different environments.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.754
We can have flow hooks,
references, target servers.

00:15:26.754 --> 00:15:28.420
But I'm going to look
at key value maps.

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:29.753
That's what's interesting to me.

00:15:29.753 --> 00:15:32.920
So I have lots of passwords
for back-end systems in here,

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.380
and I keep them stored in here.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:36.880
So this is where they all are.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:38.130
You can look them up by value.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:40.779
So look up the key, and
then you get back the value.

00:15:40.779 --> 00:15:42.820
And that's where my employee
directory, username,

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.861
and password, they're
going to be stored in here.

00:15:44.861 --> 00:15:46.114
And that's where I get them.

00:15:46.114 --> 00:15:47.530
I could have
different credentials

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:49.946
for my different environments--
my production environment,

00:15:49.946 --> 00:15:51.850
my test environment,
QA staging, whatever

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.410
my environment happens to be.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.310
I can have a map
for each of those.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:56.290
So if you have
different credentials

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:57.610
for those different
environments,

00:15:57.610 --> 00:15:59.985
we'd automatically be able to
get the right one depending

00:15:59.985 --> 00:16:01.560
on where the thing's deployed.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.850
So that's where that came from.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:09.370
So that's an example of how
we might build a simple API

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.980
proxy that would integrate
with our existing identity

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.192
management system.

00:16:14.192 --> 00:16:15.900
So that was the business
flow, and that's

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:17.577
what checks the token.

00:16:17.577 --> 00:16:18.910
But where did we get that token?

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:20.270
Where did that come from?

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:23.290
So let's take a look
at the token flow

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:26.780
and see where the token
actually came from.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:28.420
So I have another
flow in here that is

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:31.390
going to return tokens for me.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.427
And in this case,
we're going to do

00:16:34.427 --> 00:16:36.510
some checking of that
actual username and password

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.643
before we return a
token to somebody.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.480
So here's the flow for that.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:45.080
I've got a spike arrest in here.

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:46.150
And that's going
to prevent somebody

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:47.316
from calling us too quickly.

00:16:47.316 --> 00:16:49.390
But if we notice down here
under Proxy Endpoints,

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.070
we've got a few different
flows down here.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:54.940
So this pre-flow is
called all the time,

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.860
no matter how I call this proxy.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.150
And then there's conditional
things in between there.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:01.608
And then there's a post-flow
that is called unconditionally

00:17:01.608 --> 00:17:02.410
after that.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.970
In this case, if I call
this with an HTTP post

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:08.720
and I'm calling /token,
this logic gets executed.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.200
So this is the actual flow
we're going to get the token.

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:13.579
So first thing here is we're
going to authorize the user.

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:15.712
So in this case,
what we're doing is

00:17:15.712 --> 00:17:17.920
we're going to send in the
username and password that

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:18.640
was sent to us.

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.014
We're going to send that over
to the authorization system

00:17:21.014 --> 00:17:22.910
and have it check this for us.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:27.890
So the URL for the
system is down here.

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:30.167
And then here is the
actual body of the payload

00:17:30.167 --> 00:17:31.250
that I'm going to send in.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.820
So I'm going to do an HTTP post.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:36.590
I'm going to send in the
username and the password.

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:38.620
And then I'm going to
get back a response

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:42.190
and I'm going to store
that in IDM response.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.190
I will allow 15 seconds
for that to happen.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.648
If that doesn't happen, we're
going to get a timeout error.

00:17:46.648 --> 00:17:49.180
So that's the actual
call to the system.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.650
If it fails, if it returns
something that's not a 200,

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:53.939
we're going to have this fire.

00:17:53.939 --> 00:17:56.230
And it's going to say, "Invalid
username and password."

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:57.460
You can put whatever you
like in there, whatever

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:59.740
makes sense, maybe
some guidance on how

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:04.680
to deal with that or hints
on how that might work.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.060
So as long as it returns
OK, that'll be skipped

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:08.600
and we'll go on to here.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.360
So there's a session
token that's returned.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:12.920
And I want to grab
that out of there.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.010
So that will give me some
information from the identity

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:17.450
management system.

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:20.130
It's going to return a session
token and a few other things.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:21.950
But basically, what
I'm counting on here

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:24.799
is that I got back a 200 from
the identity management system.

00:18:24.799 --> 00:18:26.840
And then, if there's any
other metadata in there,

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:27.740
I'll grab that out.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:32.000
In this case, it's a session
token, and we'll take that.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.010
In this next step
here, we're going

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:36.270
to set some keys for our API.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:39.320
So that's where we're going to
set our API keys for the token

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.827
we're going to generate.

00:18:40.827 --> 00:18:42.660
And then, finally, this
generates the token.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.440
So we're doing a
password grant type.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.339
And we're going
to build a token.

00:18:48.339 --> 00:18:50.130
And we're going to
return that to the user.

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:51.838
And we'll take a look
at that in a second

00:18:51.838 --> 00:18:53.740
so we see what that
flow looks like.

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:55.270
So the username
and password are--

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:55.834
I'm sorry.

00:18:55.834 --> 00:18:57.250
The username and
the session token

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.458
are the two attributes we're
going to stick on there.

00:18:59.458 --> 00:19:01.232
When we generate a
token with Apigee Edge,

00:19:01.232 --> 00:19:03.440
you can stick any kind of
metadata you want in there.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.620
So this might be
identity information

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:08.350
that you grabbed from your
identity management system,

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:11.860
what department they work in,
a billing code, a partner ID,

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:13.210
anything like that.

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:16.330
These get populated as variables
whenever that token flows

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:17.180
through the system.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:19.400
And we can use that
for dynamic routing.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.255
We could use it for
dynamic rate limiting.

00:19:21.255 --> 00:19:22.630
We can control
what they're doing

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:24.850
and how they are routed
through the system and things

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:26.386
like that.

00:19:26.386 --> 00:19:28.690
So that generates our token.

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:29.690
So that's our two flows.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:31.220
One of them is the
business flow that's

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:32.928
going to connect to
the back-end, system,

00:19:32.928 --> 00:19:34.010
and it requires a token.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:36.480
And then this flow
is the token flow.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:39.230
And it'll generate tokens for
us so that we can send them

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.480
to the application and it
can be used to call things.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:47.360
So let's look at what those
look like from the command line,

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:48.870
so to speak.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:51.144
So in this case, here's my
identity management system.

00:19:51.144 --> 00:19:53.060
What I've got here is a
tool that lets me just

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:54.430
call REST-based end points.

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:56.420
So I'm going to call up
the identity management

00:19:56.420 --> 00:20:00.260
system, the tokens, and then
the actual business app.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:02.800
So the identity
management system,

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:04.510
what I like to do
when I'm building this

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:07.360
is I'll pull up a tool like
this and I'll prototype my API.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:10.480
So what does it take to
close that back-end system?

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.850
What can I send in there
and what will I get back?

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:14.300
And how is that going to work?

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:17.760
So if I send in a username
and password to this,

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:18.760
this is what I get back.

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:22.660
I've gotten back a 200 OK,
and that means that I'm OK.

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:26.440
If I come in here and I
change my username in there

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:30.650
and I send that in, we're going
to get back a 401 unauthorized.

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:33.070
Those are the two responses
I can get from the system.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:35.080
And the password here,
it's using a variable

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.557
so that we can't
see the password.

00:20:37.557 --> 00:20:39.640
So that's the identity
management system directly.

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:42.370
That's what we're
doing that call-out to.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:44.709
The token flow where
we're calling Apigee,

00:20:44.709 --> 00:20:46.750
we're going to send in
the username and password.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.060
It'll send it out to the
identity management system.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.280
If it gets back that
200, then it continues

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:54.802
and it returns us a
Apigee OAuth token.

00:20:54.802 --> 00:20:56.010
And that will look like this.

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:57.860
So here's our token
that comes back.

00:20:57.860 --> 00:20:59.460
We've got a few extra
things in there.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.450
We've got the user ID in there.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:03.810
We put that on there.

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:06.687
And then we've got a
session or a-- let's see.

00:21:06.687 --> 00:21:07.520
Where is it in here?

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:09.920
It's a session token.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:12.420
So there's a session token that
comes back from the identity

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:13.170
management system.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:14.790
We might use that for logging.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:17.290
What's important to me, though,
is this access token right

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:17.790
here.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.620
And I'm going to grab
that and copy it.

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.270
And then here's
our business flow.

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:25.920
So this is a simple application
that lets us look up employees.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.360
And we need to have a
basic Auth header in here.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.830
And if we don't have one, it'll
say that we're not authorized.

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.560
So we can come in here
and put a value in there.

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:40.140
And then we'll paste our
token value in there.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.910
And when we have a token,
we'll see employees come back.

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:46.270
So what this does is we can
look up an employee by name.

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:48.420
And I'm looking for
anybody named Smith here,

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:50.430
and that's what I'm
seeing come back.

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:52.230
So the flow is complete there.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:53.850
We pass in a username
and password,

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:56.250
and in exchange for that
we're given a token.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.500
And then we use that
token in the business flow

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:00.420
and we pass that as
a header on here.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.060
And that's what gets
us into the system.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:06.720
So simple example of how we
might do security mediation.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:10.382
So we're going to use one set of
credentials on the application

00:22:10.382 --> 00:22:12.840
side, and we're going to use
a different set of credentials

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:14.580
on the back-end or system side.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.839
Nice pattern for putting a
facade on an existing system.

00:22:17.839 --> 00:22:19.380
A lot of times,
dealing with security

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.360
is a big burden for developers.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:23.610
And being able to have
consistent security for all

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.901
of these things is going to
make it a lot easier to do.

00:22:30.195 --> 00:22:31.570
So let's look at
another example.

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:52.677
So a nice example, username
and password for the back end.

00:22:52.677 --> 00:22:54.260
But what if you're
using Google Cloud?

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:56.697
What if you need to have
a service account for all

00:22:56.697 --> 00:22:59.030
of your different things that
you can do in Google Cloud

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:00.738
and you need to get
credentials for that?

00:23:00.738 --> 00:23:04.610
So a somewhat more
complicated use case.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:06.539
But once we've
mastered this, it's

00:23:06.539 --> 00:23:08.330
easy to do for any
service in Google Cloud.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:12.920
So when you want to
access a system in GCP,

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.339
you need to have an
OAuth token for that.

00:23:15.339 --> 00:23:16.880
And in order to get
that OAuth token,

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:18.950
you've got to send
in a JWT request.

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.230
And it has to be signed
in a particular way.

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:23.960
And once that's sent in, we'll
get back that OAuth token.

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:25.070
It's got a timeout on it.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.944
And we can continue to
send that as a header.

00:23:26.944 --> 00:23:29.690
So same pattern here as
we saw in the last one.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:32.060
But in this case, we're
going to do an Apigee OAuth

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:34.790
token over here on
the left-hand side.

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:37.340
And then we're going
to do a GCP token over

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.842
on the right-hand side.

00:23:38.842 --> 00:23:41.300
So what we're doing in this
case is the authentication flow

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:42.350
will be exactly the same.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:44.930
We're going to
authenticate the end user.

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:47.660
And we're going to talk to the
identity management system.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:52.250
And that is going to generate
a token as that very last step

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:53.870
and then return to the client.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:55.970
And then we've got to
verify key on there

00:23:55.970 --> 00:23:57.811
as the first step in
the business flow.

00:23:57.811 --> 00:23:59.810
We're doing some rate
limiting to make sure they

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:00.884
don't call too quickly.

00:24:00.884 --> 00:24:02.300
Then we're going
to check a quota,

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.247
make sure that they
didn't exceed that quota.

00:24:04.247 --> 00:24:06.080
And we've got some
response caching in there

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:08.060
to speed things up.

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:09.810
What's new in here is
we have a thing that

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.910
says GCP Authorization.

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:13.720
And that's a shared flow.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:15.840
So what you can do
inside of Apigee Edge

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.580
is you can have a
series of flows,

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.496
and you can have some
that you'd like to share

00:24:19.496 --> 00:24:21.330
between different API proxies.

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:24.000
I build a lot of things that
talk to Google Cloud Platform,

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:28.030
so being able to reuse my
authentication is really nice.

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:29.860
So I'll put that
into a shared flow.

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:31.920
And I include that in
multiple APIs proxies

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.575
without having to
build it every time.

00:24:33.575 --> 00:24:35.700
So what we're seeing here
is there's a shared flow,

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:37.360
and there's quite a
few steps in there.

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:39.960
The last step in here is
the one that's important.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:41.544
Just like we saw in
the last example,

00:24:41.544 --> 00:24:42.960
we need to have a
header in there.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:45.600
We need to have a bearer token
and an authorization header.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.390
And that's the value that we
got back from the Google Cloud

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:49.680
identity management system.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.230
So it sends us that
token, and we're

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:54.845
going to insert a bearer
token in there as a header.

00:24:54.845 --> 00:24:56.220
And that's the
last step in here.

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.940
The other steps are
what lead to that.

00:24:57.940 --> 00:24:59.720
So I'm going to
call out to this.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.860
And the first step
in here is a cache.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:03.990
If I already have that
value in cache-- maybe

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:05.240
I got it a few minutes ago.

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:06.270
It lasts 30 minutes.

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.000
I put it in in-memory cache.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.750
I'm just going to pull
it from that cache.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:13.360
If I don't have it in cache,
the next steps are to go get it.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:16.050
So in most cases, what's
going to happen here

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:18.150
is we're going to pull
that out of cache.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:19.774
All these steps will be skipped.

00:25:19.774 --> 00:25:21.315
And then, the last
step will execute,

00:25:21.315 --> 00:25:23.400
and we'll stick that
header in there.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:25.620
If we don't already
have that value,

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.894
then all these things in
the middle do execute.

00:25:27.894 --> 00:25:30.060
So we're going to look up
some keys and secrets that

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:32.400
allow us to talk to the
identity management system.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:35.580
We've got a Java call-out
in there to build that JWT

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:37.045
and encrypt it the right way.

00:25:37.045 --> 00:25:40.920
We're going to make a call-out
to the identity management

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:42.750
system.

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:45.230
We're going to extract some
things from the response that

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:46.014
comes back.

00:25:46.014 --> 00:25:48.180
We'll adjust the timeout,
make a little bit shorter.

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:49.710
And we're going to have
the cache have a time

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:51.168
to live that's a
little bit shorter

00:25:51.168 --> 00:25:53.580
than the length of the token.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:55.650
Finally in there, if it's
not in the right format

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:58.080
or we get an error, we're going
to return an error code there.

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:00.600
We'll put it in the cache so
that next time we can find it.

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:03.800
And then we'll set a variable so
that it has a well-known value.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.300
So that's what that
flow looks like.

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:06.230
A little bit more complicated.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.360
I'll go into detail in a
minute on how this works.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.317
So end result of that
is we've got a header,

00:26:10.317 --> 00:26:11.900
and then we can call
it whatever we're

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:17.050
entitled to with that service
account inside of GCP.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:20.700
So let's look at the API proxy
and let's see how I built that.

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:30.662
So let's take a look at the
shared flow for that GCP spec.

00:26:30.662 --> 00:26:32.620
So in this case, I've
got shared flows in here.

00:26:35.077 --> 00:26:36.660
I'm going to come
in and look at that.

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:37.700
So here's GCP Auth.

00:26:40.106 --> 00:26:41.980
So when we're doing a
shared flow and Apigee,

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:43.900
this screen's a
little bit simpler.

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:46.111
And these aren't intended
to be used standalone.

00:26:46.111 --> 00:26:47.860
They're always included
in something else.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.040
They become part
of another proxy.

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.840
So let me flip over
to the Develop tab,

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.910
and we'll take a look
at what's in here.

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:57.070
So a bunch of steps in here.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.710
So first one is the cache.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:26:59.610
Do we have it cached?

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.697
If it's already cached,
just pull it out of memory

00:27:01.697 --> 00:27:02.280
and return it.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:03.700
Skip the rest of it.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.429
So that's what's in there.

00:27:05.429 --> 00:27:07.470
We're going to look for
a value called GCP token.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:08.430
That's a key for it.

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:10.710
If we don't have that, we
need to get a few things.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.418
We need to get some
credentials in there.

00:27:12.418 --> 00:27:14.910
So we've got the JWT issuer.

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:17.382
We've got the privatekey.pem.

00:27:17.382 --> 00:27:18.840
And then this is
our Java call-out.

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:22.015
So this is calling a JAR
file that's stored over here.

00:27:22.015 --> 00:27:23.640
And we're going to
pass in some values.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:27.707
We want to do RST56 for
the encryption algorithm.

00:27:27.707 --> 00:27:29.540
We're going to pass in
some values about who

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:33.710
the audience is, what
the expiration time is,

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:35.900
and then what the
claim scope is.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:37.550
So this will allow
us to call BigQuery.

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:39.300
And we're going to get
a token back that's

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:40.910
very specific to
this and gives us

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:45.390
entitlements to certain
things inside of GCP.

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:47.990
So here's the actual call-out
that will call the URL.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.799
So Google API's
Auth2 before token.

00:27:50.799 --> 00:27:52.340
So that's the way
of programmatically

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:54.590
getting a token if it's
not an interactive flow.

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:57.525
If this was an end user, they'd
be greeted by the Google login

00:27:57.525 --> 00:27:59.900
screen and they'd go through
their credentials and things

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:00.400
like.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:03.590
That this is the programmatic
way of doing that for services.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:06.320
So we will call out to that.

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:08.270
We're going to send
it as form encoded.

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:11.090
We're going to send in just the
right form parameters in there,

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:13.500
that JWT that we created.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.232
And then we're going to store
the response in token response.

00:28:16.232 --> 00:28:17.690
And we're going to
consider success

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:20.497
either a 200 or 300 code.

00:28:20.497 --> 00:28:22.830
We'll get what we need out
of there, which is the access

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:24.420
token and the expiration time.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:26.335
So that's the response
that comes back.

00:28:26.335 --> 00:28:28.710
And what we're doing is we're
actually pulling things out

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:31.090
of the response in there.

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:33.367
We'll adjust down
the expiration time

00:28:33.367 --> 00:28:35.200
and shrink it down so
it's a little bit less

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:36.940
than the expiration
of the token.

00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:38.169
There's some error handling.

00:28:38.169 --> 00:28:40.210
And then here we're going
to put it into a cache.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:42.668
So we're going to go cache that
token so that we don't have

00:28:42.668 --> 00:28:45.590
to do this every single time.

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:47.200
Finally, we'll set
it as a variable.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:49.783
And then this is the thing that
gets called every single time.

00:28:49.783 --> 00:28:52.190
This is where we actually
insert that header in there.

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:54.190
And that's what's going
to make it so that we

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:56.440
can call the back-end system.

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:58.840
So that's a typical flow of
what we might do in here.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:02.010
And that's how we would go in
and generate the GCP token.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:10.260
So let's take a step back
for a minute and talk about,

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:11.440
why are we doing all this?

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:15.180
So why not just call the
authentication system

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:15.760
every time?

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:18.390
So in particular, this flow.

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.144
Why use an Apigee token in here?

00:29:20.144 --> 00:29:21.810
Why bother with this
when you could just

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.680
call the authentication
system with every single call

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:26.730
and it would give you
a credential in there?

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:30.210
So the idea here is, when
we want to speed it up--

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:33.220
so we want to make it so that
it's faster to call the system.

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:36.355
So in each case, if we
have an Apigee token,

00:29:36.355 --> 00:29:38.230
it's about a millisecond
to check that thing.

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:39.582
So pretty fast.

00:29:39.582 --> 00:29:41.040
A call to the
authentication system

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:42.331
might take a little bit longer.

00:29:42.331 --> 00:29:45.240
But more importantly,
this Apigee token

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:47.220
that we're generating
down here has a lot

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:48.750
of metadata associated with it.

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:51.510
It's going to tell us which
API product they're using.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:53.550
It's going to tell us
who the developer is.

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:55.077
It'll have any of
those attributes

00:29:55.077 --> 00:29:56.160
that we've attached to it.

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:58.380
And with those, we can
do dynamic routing.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.880
We can send people to
different locations.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.620
We can rate limit them based
on their relationship with us.

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:06.575
So we get a lot of
additional features

00:30:06.575 --> 00:30:08.700
with that that are much
better than if we were just

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:12.029
simply checking a
username and password.

00:30:12.029 --> 00:30:13.570
So that's why we're
going to do that.

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:17.420
And that's why we're going to
cache it and do it that way.

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:19.190
So that's our two flows.

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:21.620
Let's look at an example
of an application

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:23.515
or two applications
that use these and see

00:30:23.515 --> 00:30:24.390
what those look like.

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:30.950
So the first one I showed
was the employee directory.

00:30:30.950 --> 00:30:33.740
And remember, in this case we've
got a username and password

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:36.406
for the front end that's checked
against the identity management

00:30:36.406 --> 00:30:38.780
system, and then a service
account for the back.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.822
So if I want to come in and
check my employee directory,

00:30:41.822 --> 00:30:43.530
it's going to require
me to authenticate.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:46.882
So let's say I'm searching
for somebody named Smith.

00:30:46.882 --> 00:30:48.840
Is going to say I need
a username and password.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:54.560
So I need to enter
that in there.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:57.430
And what it's going to do is
go out and get that token,

00:30:57.430 --> 00:30:59.180
and then my application
will have a token.

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:01.290
And when I search,
I get back results.

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:06.070
So if I want to search for
somebody else, I can do that.

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:09.920
I continue to search until
that token is no longer valid.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:13.060
So that's our basic
idea behind that.

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:15.130
So what does this look
like behind the scenes?

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:16.660
What's the flow for that?

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.780
This is a simple,
single-page web application.

00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:21.580
It's written in JavaScript.

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:24.640
And what I've got in here,
the interesting parts of it

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:27.230
are down here where I'm going
to go and actually call and get

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:29.480
a token and where I'm going
to do the business search.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.540
So here's the basic search.

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:34.694
We're going to do URL
in there for the API.

00:31:34.694 --> 00:31:36.610
It's going to get back
some data and then draw

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:38.050
that on the screen.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:39.730
Down here is update token.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:42.491
So that's the process for
going and getting a token.

00:31:42.491 --> 00:31:44.240
We're not storing any
credentials in here.

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.390
So this is a
JavaScript application.

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:47.320
It's in a web page.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:49.190
Somebody can easily hit
View Source on this.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:51.190
So we don't want the
credentials stored in here.

00:31:51.190 --> 00:31:54.340
We want to be able to go get
a short lived access token.

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:56.200
That access token's
only good for 30 minutes

00:31:56.200 --> 00:32:00.080
or whatever we set that
session time out to.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:03.830
So that's the application
for the password on the front

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:06.900
and the service
account on the back.

00:32:06.900 --> 00:32:09.600
This application is
going to call BigQuery.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.750
So what I've got in here
is an application where

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:14.160
I can check historical weather.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.650
There's a few sample
data sets for BigQuery.

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:19.810
One of them is historical
weather for North America.

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:21.780
So I can look at
weather for any city

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:24.690
and I can check what the weather
has been for the last 100 years

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:25.657
or so.

00:32:25.657 --> 00:32:27.990
What I did in this case was
I looked up at San Francisco

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:30.330
and I wanted to see what the
weather was like in 2015.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:32.220
So two things are
happening in here.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.350
When I call this, it's
going to do some geocoding

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.470
and look up the location
that I put in here.

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:39.810
And then it's going to return
a list of weather stations

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:41.340
that are in that area.

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:43.200
I can supply it with
a year, and then it's

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.158
going to go and retrieve
weather for that data.

00:32:45.158 --> 00:32:47.730
So this showing me high
temperature data from January

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:49.650
all the way through December.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:53.160
So what's interesting in here
is I'm sending in an API key

00:32:53.160 --> 00:32:55.365
on this, and then it's
doing that flow in there

00:32:55.365 --> 00:32:57.030
where it's going
to go get a token.

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:59.430
It's going to have
a service call-out,

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:02.160
and it's going to get a token
that's appropriate for calling

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.050
BigQuery with read only access.

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:06.859
So that's what's happening
behind the scenes in here.

00:33:06.859 --> 00:33:09.150
I've applied some rate limiting
to this so if I call it

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:11.712
too quickly it's going to shut
me down and things like that.

00:33:11.712 --> 00:33:13.670
But I get everything I
need in the application.

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:14.753
It's a nice mix of things.

00:33:14.753 --> 00:33:17.520
I could authenticate people who
are going to call this however

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:18.210
I like.

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:20.910
So there's my rate limiting
kicking in to prevent somebody

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.164
from calling up to quickly.

00:33:23.164 --> 00:33:24.830
But that's the general
idea behind this.

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:35.040
So that's our applications.

00:33:37.822 --> 00:33:39.530
And that's what they
look like and that's

00:33:39.530 --> 00:33:41.834
how you could build
something like this.

