00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:06.390
So first question I've
got is, "Can you integrate

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:07.765
with Active Directory?"

00:36:07.765 --> 00:36:08.640
So I hear that a lot.

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.950
A lot of people are standardized
on Active Directory.

00:36:10.950 --> 00:36:14.380
So if you can reach your Active
Directory, we can work with it.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:18.450
So as long as you can reach it
through HTTP or a Java call-out

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:21.390
or JavaScript or Python,
you can absolutely

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:25.620
integrate with Active Directory.

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:28.030
The next question, I
guess somewhat related,

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:29.740
"What if our identity
management system

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:31.360
doesn't have an HTTP interface?

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:32.630
Will this still work?"

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:35.632
So what do we do in
the case of that?

00:36:35.632 --> 00:36:36.340
A lot of things--

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:38.800
I'm thinking like
Oracle Access Manager,

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:40.180
Tivoli, some of those--

00:36:40.180 --> 00:36:43.120
they don't necessarily
have an HTTP interface.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:45.812
If they have a Java library,
you can use that, call an API,

00:36:45.812 --> 00:36:47.770
as long as you can get
back some sort of signal

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:50.380
from that back-end thing
that says this user is OK.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.600
So if there's really
any way to call it,

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:55.190
we can use this same pattern
to integrate with that identity

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:55.940
management system.

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:01.460
"What about 2 factor
authentication?"

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:03.110
So what if you're using
2 factor authentication?

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:04.940
You've got to enter
a text code or you're

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:07.310
using Google Authenticator
or some other system that's

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:09.600
going to ask you for that
second piece of information.

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:10.800
How do you do that?

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:12.906
Well, same story in here.

00:37:12.906 --> 00:37:14.780
When we call that identity
management system,

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:17.649
it will usually push out that
second factor to the user,

00:37:17.649 --> 00:37:19.190
and then they can
enter it into here.

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:20.940
We'd need to provide
an interface for them

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:23.940
to be able to enter it or
some way to respond to that.

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:26.360
But no problem with 2
factor authentication,

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:28.580
and definitely encouraged
to keep things more secure.

